## STATE AS OF HANDOFF (2026-08-17) — §32 REFRAMES THE FRICTION LINE

* **§32 — §30 MEASURED ACCELERATION, NOT FRICTION.** On a level floor `a = mu*g` with
  g fixed, so corr(mu, decel) = 0.99976 and `a` predicts mu at R2 = 0.995 with no
  fitting. The `incline_set_v1` build breaks the identity (corr -0.65) and is the
  set to probe. **Rendering PAUSED at 36/150**, all checks green, resumable.
* **§32a — three rendering bugs**, one of which (the frame-0 leak, mu readable from
  the first frame at R2 = 1.000) would have voided the set silently. Found by looking
  at the clips, not by any validator. Both failure modes are now checks in code.
* **§31 — mu is bound to the OBJECT, not the surface**, causally: per token the cube
  is 300-800x more effective at moving the other region's readout than the floor is.
  The floor pool's decodability is inherited through attention.
* **§31a — `collide_set_v1`** (two cubes, shared floor) built and validated, PAUSED at
  21/150, superseded in priority.
* **§33 — a taxonomy** of physical variables across five levels, with the finding that
  level 5 (scene structure: attachment, support, causation) is untouched by us and by
  the field, and a ranked list of seven candidate variables.
* **§33a — Joseph et al. probe multi-object with GLOBAL mean-pooling**, per their own
  text; no token attribution is described anywhere in the paper.

**NOTHING IN THIS SESSION IS COMMITTED.** Five generators, four validators,
`steer_friction.py`, `patch_surface.py`, `analyse_collide_friction.py`, the `DERIVED`
additions and this file are all single-copy on a box that has already lost its OptiX
cache to a rebuild once.

